+// Uniform zoom of the guest output rectangle about the surface centre, applied
+// AFTER the fit/crop math below. It exists because that math can only ever
+// SHRINK its rectangle: it starts from "stretched across the whole surface on
+// the long axis" and letterboxes back toward the safe area, so no safe-area
+// value can make the output bigger than the surface on that axis. iOS "Screen:
+// fill width" needs exactly that -- scaling the game's own ~2.38:1 WIDE
+// letterbox band up to the height of a 2.17:1 screen requires an output
+// rectangle wider than the surface, with the sides cropped. Overflow on either
+// axis (negative output_x / output_y) is the same state the safe-area cutoff
+// already produces on one axis, and is handled identically downstream.
+REXCVAR_DEFINE_INT32(present_zoom_percent, 100, "UI/Presenter",
+                     "Zoom of the guest output rectangle about the surface centre, percent "
+                     "(100 = the plain fit/crop rectangle)")
+    .range(100, 400)
+    .lifecycle(rex::cvar::Lifecycle::kHotReload);
 
